## 5. First unproved theorem, stated exactly

The first obligation on the critical path that is **not** proved is
obligation (d), critical-A Frobenius versus prime-main-term alignment,
including the source A-to-G error transport. In the source it is exactly

```agda
-- DASHI/Analysis/RiemannG3SourceTransportExact.agda, lines 227–235
record G3RemainingAlignment (owned : G3SourceOwnedTransport) : Set₁ where
  field
    CriticalFiniteABaseline           : Set
    RetainedDefectInsideAFrobenius    : Set
    CriticalBaselineEqualsPrimeMain   : Set

    criticalFiniteABaseline           : CriticalFiniteABaseline
    retainedDefectInsideAFrobenius    : RetainedDefectInsideAFrobenius
    criticalBaselineEqualsPrimeMain   : CriticalBaselineEqualsPrimeMain
```

This is a record of three **abstract `Set`s together with assumed inhabitants**.
It states nothing and proves nothing: any three types with any three elements
inhabit it. No instance of `G3RemainingAlignment` is constructed for zeta data,
and none can be, because the mathematical content it is standing in for is

1. `criticalFiniteABaseline` — a finite-A archimedean/critical baseline for the
   Weil quadratic form, i.e. an explicit-formula evaluation at the tapered test
   function with a two-sided bound on the archimedean term;
2. `retainedDefectInsideAFrobenius` — the statement that the retained transverse
   defect of §3(b) is dominated by the Frobenius (zero-side) contribution at
   critical A, which requires a second-moment bound on the zero side;
3. `criticalBaselineEqualsPrimeMain` — the identification of that baseline with
   the prime main term `∑_{p,m} (log p) p^{-m/2} φ̂(m log p)`.

All three require the **Weil/Riemann explicit formula for ζ** together with
prime-side second-moment estimates. Neither exists in this bundle nor in
Mathlib: Mathlib has `riemannZeta`, its functional equation, and nonvanishing on
`Re s ≥ 1`, but no explicit formula and no Weil quadratic form.

The next unproved obligation after (d) is (e), valid localisation/amplification
with bounded-height closure, in
`DASHI/Analysis/RiemannHermitianLocalizationAmplificationExact.agda`: as
committed (and as repaired) its localisation and amplification statements are
again `ℕ`-ledger identities, and the bounded-height closure step depends on (d).

